-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Non-Planar Faces:** In a curvilinear grid, quadrilateral faces may not be planar. A viewing ray can leave a cell and re-enter it across the same face, making a strict visibility sort impossible for that face [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Occlusion Outside Pixel Centers:** The algorithm does not produce a true visibility sort because cells may occlude each other along viewing rays which do not pass through pixel centers. However, since the A-buffer rays are the same as the pixel sample rays used in the final hardware rendering, the image will be correct [S1].
+**Parameters and Edge Cases:**
+*   **Sorting Heuristic:** Sorting by centroid depth is critical for performance. If faces are not sorted, list insertion becomes $O(k)$ per fragment where $k$ is the number of fragments per pixel, degrading performance [S1][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Non-Planar Faces:** In curvilinear grids, quadrilateral faces may be non-planar. A viewing ray can leave and re-enter the same cell across the same face, making a strict visibility sort impossible for that specific ray. The A-buffer handles this by recording multiple intersections for the same face if they occur at different depths, but the resulting topological sort may contain cycles or ambiguities if not handled by the general topological sort algorithm's cycle detection or if the geometry is degenerate [S1][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Approximation:** The method does not produce a true global visibility sort because occlusions along rays not passing through pixel centers are ignored. However, since the A-buffer rays match the pixel sample rays used in final hardware rendering, the resulting image is correct [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [ZSWEEP](1280-zsweep.md)
-ZSWEEP is an exact, object-order cell projection algorithm for volume rendering on unstructured grids that optimizes for sorting efficiency and memory usage. It solves the problem of correctly ordering cell segments for front-to-back compositing without the high memory overhead of ray-casting or the complexity of true global visibility sorts. It sets itself apart from its parent [A-buffer visibility sort for non-convex grids](1057-a-buffer-visibility-sort-for-non-convex-grids.md) by using a sweep-plane approach that exploits the implicit global ordering induced by vertex z-coordinates, rather than maintaining full A-buffer lists for all pixels simultaneously. Choose it when memory is constrained and vertex-based ordering is sufficient.
